    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey prof, i'm stuck on this signal processing homework. i get the formula for the fourier transform but i dont really get the actual intuition behind it. like what am i actually doing to the signal when i transform it? can u explain it in a way that makes sense physically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Think of the Fourier Transform as a complete spectral fingerprint of a signal. Instead of looking at a signal's amplitude over *time* (what you see normally), it lets you see the signal's strength across *every possible frequency*. Essentially, you are decomposing a complex waveform into a sum of pure sine and cosine waves, telling you exactly which frequencies make up the whole mess. This is useful because different frequencies represent different components, like low bass versus high treble in sound.",
