    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay that makes sense. so if i have a messy signal with a lot of random noise, the transform just separates the useful parts from the noise? how does it actually \"find\" those frequencies though? like is there some kind of mechanism that isolates them?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The mechanism that \"finds\" the frequencies is through mathematical correlation, which is the core of the transformation itself. The transform essentially tests the input signal against countless pure sine wave \"templates\" of every possible frequency. Where the signal strongly matches a specific template, the output energy for that frequency will be high, effectively isolating that component. High randomness or noise usually spreads its energy across many frequencies, making the desired, periodic signal stand out distinctly in the frequency domain.",
      "source": "target",
